-- Mount Fernow Southeast Face / Leroy Basin (wa_mount_fernow_southeast_face):
-- dist_km corrected 8 -> 14.16 (was ~half the route's own itinerary total; itinerary day miles
-- sum to 17.6mi round trip [5.4+6.9+5.3], matching the itinerary's own totalNote '~17.5-mile
-- round-trip' -- one-way per app convention is half of that: 8.8mi = 14.16km).
UPDATE routes SET dist_km = 14.16 WHERE id = 'wa_mount_fernow_southeast_face';
-- bivy pruned 7->2, removing five entries (Myrtle Lake/Cow Creek Meadows, Larch Lakes meadow camps,
-- Snow Brushy Creek junction, Fox Camp, and the Emerald-Saska basin) that are explicitly and solely
-- for other Entiat-corridor peaks (Fifth of July Mountain, Cardinal/Emerald/Saska/Pinnacle Mountain)
-- with zero mention of Fernow anywhere in their text -- same regional bivy-list contamination
-- pattern documented in many prior batches. Kept Cottonwood Camp and Entiat Meadows, both of which
-- this route's own approach/beta text explicitly names as alternate eastern approaches to Fernow.
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 3140, "name": "Cottonwood Camp and the Entiat River road campgrounds", "type": "camp", "notes": "This is the road head for the whole eastern side of the Chelan Mountains crest and the standard place to sleep the night before a long walk in. The drive is the thing people underestimate — it is roughly 38 miles of valley road from the Columbia, most of it paved, and getting here from the west side of the state is the better part of four hours. Cottonwood sits at the end, just short of where the Entiat River trail begins. Camp here and you can be walking at first light with the entire day for the eleven miles to Entiat Meadows or the eight to Fox Camp. Recognise what has happened to this valley: successive fires have run through it and the lower trail corridor is open, burned, dusty and hot, with young growth and standing snags rather than shade. That makes the river the only dependable water for the first several miles and it makes an early start a safety measure rather than a preference. A landslide near the river trailhead currently blocks the normal approach; a temporary high-clearance route has been pushed through the slide, trailers are turned back, and the campground short of it is being used as the turnaround. Confirm before committing to the drive, because there is nothing at the end of this road.", "water": "The Entiat River runs beside every one of these campgrounds and is the most reliable water anywhere in the zone. Treat it. Some of the smaller road campgrounds have a hand pump but several have none at all and nothing should be assumed potable.", "permit": "Northwest Forest Pass or equivalent recreation pass for the trailhead lot. No permit to camp. Forest-wide Stage 2 fire restrictions have prohibited campfires on all national forest land since early July 2026, including in established rings, and the county has a total outdoor burn ban. All of these campgrounds are inside the current fire closure order through 31 October 2026, and Cottonwood is written off for the remainder of the season.", "capacity": "Cottonwood has around two dozen sites; the string of small campgrounds down-valley adds a similar number again"}, {"elev": 4600, "name": "Entiat Meadows", "type": "camp", "notes": "The head of the Entiat valley and the reason to walk this trail at all: eleven miles up, entering the glacier drainage with Mount Fernow, Seven Fingered Jack and Mount Maude standing directly above. It serves two peaks. It is the eastern base for Mount Fernow, from which a party crosses toward the Entiat Glacier and takes the east ridge onto the southeast face — a longer and much less travelled line than the Leroy Basin scramble that most people use from the Chiwawa side, and one that should be chosen deliberately rather than by accident. It is also a reasonable if roundabout base for Spectacle Buttes, whose usual approaches leave the same river trail lower down by way of the Ice Creek or Cool Creek trails. Be clear-eyed about the commitment: eleven miles in with a mountaineering load is a full day, the first several miles are burned and hot, and there is no shortening it. The meadow itself is one of the finest places in the eastern Cascades in late July when the flowers are out, and it is high enough and shaded enough to be cold at night even in midsummer. Bring the right shell. The glacier above has retreated substantially and should not be assumed to be where an old map puts it.", "water": "The infant Entiat River runs through the meadow directly off the glacier above, so water is abundant, cold and dependable through the whole season. This is the strongest water in the zone. Above the meadows, on the climb toward the glacier and the east ridge, there is nothing until you reach snow, so fill completely at camp.", "permit": "Glacier Peak Wilderness — free, self-issued at the trailhead, party size limited to twelve. No campfires under the current forest-wide restrictions; at this elevation in a hanging valley there is little dead wood worth burning anyway. Inside the current fire closure order through 31 October 2026.", "capacity": "First camp is a quarter mile into the meadow, tucked into trees on the left; several more sites scattered up-valley. Room for two or three parties spread out."}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_mount_fernow_southeast_face';

