-- Raven Ridge Southeast Ridge / Crater Lake Approach (wa_raven_ridge_southeast_ridge_crater_lake):
-- itinerary converted from a bare narrative string to the standard {cal,days,totalNote} object shape
-- every other route in the catalog uses (confirmed against the app's own seed-data shape and against
-- RouteDetail.jsx, which reads route.itinerary.days.length to decide whether to render the day-by-day
-- plan) -- the existing narrative text is preserved verbatim as totalNote, days is an honest empty
-- array since no day-by-day breakdown was ever recorded, no content invented.
UPDATE routes SET itinerary = '{"cal": "", "totalNote": "The documented winter ascent (Gilbertson & Robinson, Jan 2019) skied in from an unplowed road to camp at Crater Lake, summited and returned to camp the same day, then skied/hiked out the next day. A summer visit via the maintained Crater Creek/Crater Lakes Trail (7.8 miles round trip to the lake) could reasonably be done as a single long day or a lighter two-day trip with a camp at the lake.", "days": []}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_raven_ridge_southeast_ridge_crater_lake';
-- bivy pruned 8->2, removing six entries (Scatter Lake basin/Abernathy Peak, Reynolds Creek upper
-- basin/Reynolds Peak, Oval Lakes basin/Oval-Courtney-Star, Boiling Lake basin/Star-Courtney, Prince
-- Creek Campground/Star-Courtney-Oval, Bird Creek Camp/Devore-Tupshin -- the last of which explicitly
-- self-disqualifies: 'these two are not a Sawtooth trip... everything about them is different from
-- the Twisp River peaks') that are explicitly and solely for other Sawtooth/Chelan-corridor peaks with
-- zero mention of Raven Ridge. Kept Libby Lake ('the camp for BOTH Raven Ridge routes') and Upper Eagle
-- Lake/Horsehead Pass ('Hoodoo and Raven Ridge also within reach along the crest'), both of which name
-- Raven Ridge directly.
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 7618, "name": "Libby Lake", "type": "camp", "notes": "The camp for both Raven Ridge routes and the usual base for Hoodoo Peak. Getting here is a different drive from the Twisp River trailheads: from Twisp go east on Highway 20 then south on Highway 153 about nine miles and turn up the Libby Creek road. The trail is a steep 5.3 miles to a lake at roughly 7,600 ft, gaining well over 3,000 ft, and it passes the remains of an old cabin at around four miles that makes a lower alternative camp if you run out of daylight or want water lower down. Raven Ridge rises about 800 ft directly above the lake, and the standard gully-and-ridge line starts from the shore, so this is as close as a camp gets to a route in this range. Hoodoo is a scramble from the same basin, and strong parties climb both in a day from here. The upper basin holds snow into July and the gullies can be firm and unpleasant early; late August through the first half of October is the good window, and the larch here is as good as anywhere in the Sawtooth. It is exposed and treeless at the lake, so a camp below the outlet is more comfortable in wind.", "water": "The lake, and Libby Creek along the trail below. The last several hundred feet of the climb are dry.", "permit": "Forest Service, no overnight permit. Northwest Forest Pass or Interagency pass at the Libby Lake trailhead. This is a high, sparse, slow-growing basin; carry a stove and do not build a fire, and expect a formal restriction across the lower Methow through the summer.", "capacity": "A few small sites below the lake, and rougher ground around it. Room for two or three parties without crowding; the shoreline is fragile and there is little flat ground."}, {"elev": 7110, "name": "Upper Eagle Lake and Horsehead Pass", "type": "camp", "notes": "The hub of the southern Sawtooth and the practical base for Switchback Mountain, with Hoodoo and Raven Ridge also within reach along the crest. Access is the Gold Creek side, not the Twisp River: turn off the highway onto the North Fork Gold Creek road, then a signed spur for several miles to the Eagle Lakes trailhead at about 4,760 ft. It is roughly 12.5 miles round trip to Upper Eagle Lake at 7,110 ft with only about 2,350 ft of gain, so it is a gentle carry by the standards of this range. Lower Eagle Lake at about 6,490 ft is the quieter option. Horsehead Pass at roughly 7,540 ft sits just above camp and is the gateway both to Boiling Lake on the far side and to the ridge walking that reaches Switchback. Linking on to Martin Lakes and Cooney Lake makes a longer circuit that takes in more of the southern summits. Season is roughly July into October; the pass holds snow on its north side later than the approach suggests. Be aware that Upper Eagle can be crowded and noisy on a fine weekend - if you want quiet, camp at Lower Eagle or push over the pass.", "water": "Both Eagle Lakes, and Eagle Creek is bridged on the way up. Above the lakes, on the pass and along the crest, there is nothing; carry water for any traverse day.", "permit": "Forest Service, no overnight permit needed. Northwest Forest Pass or Interagency pass at the Eagle Lakes / Crater Creek trailhead. This trail is open to motorcycles and stock as well as walkers, which surprises people. Fires are restricted most summers and firewood at 7,000 ft is not a renewable resource here.", "capacity": "Many sites - almost every level spot around Upper Eagle Lake gets used, plus a large horse camp just below it and further sites at Lower Eagle Lake about a mile off the main trail. This is the busiest backcountry camping in the range."}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_raven_ridge_southeast_ridge_crater_lake';

-- Remmel Mountain NW Ridge (wa_remmel_mountain_nw_ridge):
-- loss_ft corrected 1200 -> 5265 to match gain_ft: this route's own approach/itinerary/corrections
-- text describe a genuine out-and-back (bushwhack from Remmel Lake to a 7,500ft camp, summit day, then
-- return to Remmel Lake the same way), so loss should equal gain for a round trip to the same
-- trailhead. The old value (1200) exactly matches only the summit-to-camp PARTIAL descent
-- (8,685 - 7,500 = 1,185, rounds to 1,200) -- the same 'a partial sub-segment figure was saved as if
-- it were the route total' bug documented in batch 289's Phantom Peak fix.
UPDATE routes SET loss_ft = 5265 WHERE id = 'wa_remmel_mountain_nw_ridge';
-- outing_shape set to 'outback' (was null), consistent with the above -- a genuine round trip to the
