UPDATE routes SET outing_shape = 'outback' WHERE id = 'wa_remmel_mountain_nw_ridge';
-- bivy pruned 6->2, removing four entries (Horseshoe Basin/Windy Peak, the Berk Creek junction/Carru-
-- Lago-Lost Peak, the high bench SW of Shellrock Pass/Carru-Lago, Dot Lake and Butte Pass basins/Lost
-- Peak) that are explicitly and solely for other Pasayten peaks with zero mention of Remmel. Kept Four
-- Point Lake ('the Chewuch-side base for Remmel Mountain') and the lower Chewuch River camps out of
-- Thirtymile (staging camps toward Remmel access), both of which name Remmel directly.
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 6850, "name": "Four Point Lake — the Chewuch-side base for Remmel Mountain", "type": "camp", "notes": "The base for REMMEL MOUNTAIN, an 8,685 ft pile of granite carrying an old lookout site and one of the great viewpoints of the wilderness. The line in is the Chewuch trail from the Thirtymile trailhead out of Winthrop — a long, gentle valley walk of easy miles past Chewuch Falls with dispersed camping most of the way, to a signed junction at about thirteen miles for the Four Point Lake and Coleman Ridge trail. That trail FORDS THE CHEWUCH RIVER almost immediately and it is the crux of the approach: early in the season with snowmelt running it is genuinely difficult and there is not always a log. Past the ford the grade steepens sharply and about two miles of climbing reaches the lake at 6,850 ft, with a spur into the basin. From camp the summit goes on open ground by way of the Coleman Ridge side, and it is a scramble rather than a climb. Reckon on thirty-five miles and six thousand feet round trip over three or four days. THE LOWER VALLEY IS BURN, repeatedly, from fires in the early 2000s and since, so expect hot open walking, no shade and standing dead timber. This approach is chosen over the Remmel Lake side when the Andrews Creek road is shut, which in recent seasons has been often.", "water": "The lake, and it is dependable through the season. This is the last certain water on the approach — the ridge above it carries nothing once the snow has gone, so fill here for the summit day rather than hoping.", "permit": "Free self-issue wilderness permit at the Thirtymile trailhead kiosk, no quota and no reservation. Northwest Forest Pass or interagency pass to park. Camp back from the shoreline. Weed-free feed for stock and no tying animals to trees overnight; this is heavily used horse country and the volunteers who keep the trail open are mostly equestrians. Fires legal in existing rings outside a ban, and bans are usual by late summer in this drainage.", "capacity": "Several good sites around the lake with open views to the ridge and the peak, room for two or three parties without stacking up. Lightly used, because almost everyone approaching Remmel comes the other way."}, {"name": "The lower Chewuch River camps out of Thirtymile", "type": "camp", "notes": "These exist to break the walk rather than to serve a summit, and on an approach this long that is worth more than it sounds. The Chewuch trail is gentle enough that a party can cover a lot of it quickly, but thirteen miles to the Four Point junction with a full pack is a poor first day after the drive from the west side, and the river ford above is better met in the morning with fresh legs and lower water. Stopping around the falls at six or seven miles makes the second day short and puts you at Four Point Lake by early afternoon with the summit still available. IT IS ALSO THE PIECE OF THIS ZONE MOST LIKELY TO BE OPEN. When the fires shut Iron Gate, Andrews Creek and the Harts Pass road, this corridor stayed passable and volunteers kept clearing it, which is why it is written up separately rather than folded into the Four Point entry. Expect burned forest for long stretches, little shade, and a trail whose blowdown situation changes with every windstorm. Ask the ranger district before building a schedule on it.", "water": "Good, and unusually so for this wilderness. The Chewuch runs beside the trail for the whole of the lower valley and every one of these sites has water at it into the autumn. Water only becomes a problem once you leave the river for the climb to Four Point Lake.", "permit": "Free self-issue Pasayten permit at the trailhead kiosk, no quota, no reservation. Northwest Forest Pass or interagency pass to park at Thirtymile. Weed-free feed for stock; animals may not be tied to trees overnight or highlined close to them. Fires in existing rings outside a burn ban, and this is dry east-side country where bans are the norm from midsummer.", "capacity": "Dispersed sites scattered along the first several miles of the valley, including good ground near Chewuch Falls. Most take one or two tents and they are seldom occupied — this is a corridor rather than a destination."}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_remmel_mountain_nw_ridge';

-- Ridge Traverse from East Fury (wa_ridge_traverse_from_east_fury):
-- access._raw removed: it named 'Limited permits for Elk Lake and Glacier Meadows basecamp' and 'No
-- camping between Glacier Meadows and Blue Glacier' -- Elk Lake, Glacier Meadows and the Blue Glacier
-- are all Mount Olympus / Olympic National Park features (confirmed: Mount Fury sits in the Picket
-- Range, North Cascades National Park, on the opposite side of the state) -- a clear cross-region
-- contamination of the kind documented repeatedly in this project. The top-level access fields
-- (fees/notes/rules/closures/landManager) are already correct, Fury-specific content and are
-- unaffected; only the stale/wrong _raw sub-object is dropped.
UPDATE routes SET access = '{"fees": "N/A", "notes": "60% of sites are reservable in advance via Recreation.gov (2026 lottery Mar 2–13); the remaining 40% are walk-up, obtained in person the day before at the Wilderness Information Center in Marblemount.", "rules": "Group size capped at 6 in off-trail cross-country zones (which includes most Picket Range approaches; 12 in on-trail corridors). Bear canisters required in some zones — free loaners at permit offices.", "closures": "Best June-August; winter snow closure typical Nov-May; Glacier Meadows permits limited and first-come first-served. Luna Fire closure, read from the NPS North Cascades fire-closures page on 17 September 2026 (page last updated 14 September 2026): the Big Beaver Trail is closed from Big Beaver Stock Camp to Stillwell Camp, and Luna Camp, Beaver Pass Camp (with its stock and group camps) and 39 Mile Camp (with 39 Mile Stock) are closed; cross-country zone access from closed trails is prohibited. This is a dated snapshot of a moving closure - re-read the NPS closure page before relying on it.", "group_limit": 6, "landManager": "National Park Service (North Cascades National Park)", "land_manager": "National Park Service — North Cascades National Park (Stephen Mather Wilderness)"}'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_ridge_traverse_from_east_fury';

