-- gain_ft/loss_ft=7000/7000 -- one-way per app convention is half of 14mi: 7mi = 11.27km.
UPDATE routes SET dist_km = 11.27 WHERE id = 'wa_robinson_mountain_north_couloir';
-- bivy pruned 8->1, removing seven entries (Harts Pass/Meadows campgrounds, Windy Pass, Hopkins
-- Lake/Castle Pass, Doris Lake/Freds Lake basins, Shellrock Pass, Lake of the Woods, Copper Glance
-- Lake) that are explicitly and solely for other Pasayten peaks (Osceola/Blackcap/Dot/Ptarmigan/
-- Castle/Blizzard/Monument/Lake Mountain/Big-West Craggy) with zero mention of Robinson Mountain --
-- one entry (Windy Pass) even self-disqualifies outright: 'As pure staging these camps do nothing
-- for the Robinson Creek or Middle Fork peaks, which go the other direction from the pass.' Kept
-- only 'Robinson Creek valley campsites', which explicitly describes this peak's own approach.
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"name": "Robinson Creek valley campsites", "type": "camp", "notes": "The trailhead sits at about 2,560 feet just off the Harts Pass road, which means this is the low road into country whose summits stand near 8,500 — the elevation gain is the whole story. Robinson Mountain is climbed from here by walking a little over two miles to the Beauty Creek bridge and picking up an unmaintained path just beyond it, then leaving that for open slopes above; it is fourteen miles round trip with over six thousand feet of gain and only very fit parties do it in a day, so a first night at one of the lower established sites is the sensible plan. Carrying on up the valley reaches Robinson Pass at 6,220 feet in about nine and a half miles and drops into the Middle Fork Pasayten, which is the back door to Blackcap and Osceola for anyone who would rather not start from Slate Pass. The lower valley burns and reburns, so expect open hot walking, standing dead timber and variable blowdown, and note that this drainage was inside the 2026 fire closure. Ask the district about trail conditions before building a schedule around it.", "water": "Good, and unusually so for this wilderness — the creek runs beside the trail for most of its length and most of the established sites hold water into the autumn. This is the one approach in the zone where water is not the limiting factor.", "permit": "Free self-issue Pasayten permit at the trailhead kiosk, no quota and no reservation. Northwest Forest Pass or Interagency pass to park. Certified weed-free feed is required for stock and animals may not be tied directly to trees overnight or highlined within eight feet of them. Campfires are allowed in existing rings outside a burn ban, though the lower valley is dry and bans are common by late summer.", "capacity": "Nine established sites are spread along the trail at roughly one and a half, two and a half, four, six, six and a half, seven and a half and nine miles from the trailhead, so a party can pick its distance rather than racing to one spot. Most take one or two tents. Stock parties use this valley heavily."}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_robinson_mountain_north_couloir';

